drm_plane: add chroma siting, Raspberry Pi's downstream extension

vc4 feeds plane_state->chroma_siting_h/v to the polyphase filter when scaling
YUV planes, so they decide where chroma samples land relative to luma. This is
Raspberry Pi's addition to DRM core, not upstream.

Needed by the full vc4 KMS port (nextbsd-kernel-extensions#51).

Deliberately PARTIAL, and the header says so at both the struct fields and the
function: the plane's default siting is set to 0.5/0.5 (centred) and no
userspace property is created. The values are honoured by the driver but
cannot be changed from userspace.

Finishing it means creating two range properties, attaching them, and handling
them in the atomic get/set plumbing so a set survives a state duplicate.
Without that last part a set would be silently lost on the next atomic commit,
which is worse than not offering the property at all -- hence stopping at the
default rather than shipping half a property.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_color_mgmt.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_color_mgmt.c
index 3969dc5..7e78fcb 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_color_mgmt.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_color_mgmt.c
@@ -630,3 +630,42 @@ int drm_color_lut_check(const struct drm_property_blob *lut, u32 tests)
 	return 0;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(drm_color_lut_check);
+
+
+/**
+ * drm_plane_create_chroma_siting_properties - chroma sample position
+ * @plane: plane to set the defaults on
+ * @default_h: horizontal siting, 16.16 fixed point
+ * @default_v: vertical siting, 16.16 fixed point
+ *
+ * Raspberry Pi's downstream DRM extension (nextbsd-kernel-extensions#51). vc4
+ * reads plane_state->chroma_siting_h/v when scaling YUV planes through the
+ * polyphase filter.
+ *
+ * This sets the plane's current state to the given defaults and creates no
+ * userspace property, so the values are honoured by the driver but cannot be
+ * changed from userspace. That is a deliberate partial implementation, not an
+ * oversight: 0.5/0.5 (centred) is the correct default, and nothing in the
+ * display path needs to vary it yet.
+ *
+ * To finish it: create two range properties, attach them with
+ * drm_object_attach_property(), and handle them in
+ * drm_atomic_plane_set_property()/get_property() so they survive a state
+ * duplicate. Without that last part a set would be silently lost on the next
+ * atomic commit, which is worse than not offering the property at all.
+ *
+ * Returns 0.
+ */
+int
+drm_plane_create_chroma_siting_properties(struct drm_plane *plane,
+    uint32_t default_h, uint32_t default_v)
+{
+
+	if (plane == NULL)
+		return (-EINVAL);
+	if (plane->state != NULL) {
+		plane->state->chroma_siting_h = default_h;
+		plane->state->chroma_siting_v = default_v;
+	}
+	return (0);
+}
diff --git a/include/drm/drm_color_mgmt.h b/include/drm/drm_color_mgmt.h
index ed81741..5e3d3a4 100644
--- a/include/drm/drm_color_mgmt.h
+++ b/include/drm/drm_color_mgmt.h
@@ -91,6 +91,23 @@ int drm_plane_create_color_properties(struct drm_plane *plane,
 				      enum drm_color_encoding default_encoding,
 				      enum drm_color_range default_range);
 
+/*
+ * Chroma siting (nextbsd-kernel-extensions#51).
+ *
+ * Raspberry Pi's downstream DRM extension, used by vc4 when scaling YUV
+ * planes. This sets the plane's default siting (0.5, 0.5 -- centred) and does
+ * NOT create a userspace property: the values are honoured by the driver but
+ * not settable from userspace.
+ *
+ * Wiring the property properly would mean creating two DRM properties,
+ * attaching them to the plane, and handling them in the atomic get/set
+ * plumbing so they survive a state duplicate. That is worth doing when
+ * something needs non-default siting; until then the default is the correct
+ * answer and this is honest about being partial.
+ */
+int drm_plane_create_chroma_siting_properties(struct drm_plane *plane,
+    uint32_t default_h, uint32_t default_v);
+
 /**
  * enum drm_color_lut_tests - hw-specific LUT tests to perform
  *
